{"reference":"Jeremiah 30","book":"Jeremiah","chapter":30,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Reading Jeremiah 30 within its larger collection","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Jeremiah 30 begins a larger restoration collection in chapters 30–33. Across those chapters, healing and return stand alongside images of grief, rebuilding, replanting, Davidic hope, forgiveness, and covenant renewal; the collection does not treat restoration as erasing prior loss. This broader setting helps hold together this chapter’s severe language of wounds, fear, correction, and judgment with its promises of return, healing, rebuilding, security, and renewed belonging.","verse_refs":["Jeremiah 30:1-24"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_jeremiah_030_ac8d6e08d649a6536caf6dbf"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"The shape of the book","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"The received book of Jeremiah brings together varied forms of material, including poetry, prose sermons, narratives, letters, headings, repeated accounts, restoration collections, oracles concerning nations, and an appendix. A practical outline places chapters 30–33 within the restoration collection, though the book’s long compositional and textual history remains a matter of interpretation.","provenance_refs":["reader_path_jeremiah_030_b304a2fdcf3bc06133ae61cd"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}